    "reading_text": "The city of Venice is built on more than 100 small islands in the marshy lagoon along the Adriatic Sea. The canals, which are up to 10 meters wide and 5 meters deep, were originally dug as a means of transportation. Over time, they have become an integral part of the city's unique character. In fact, the canals are so narrow that only small boats can navigate them. This has led to the development of a unique system of bridges and walkways that connect the different islands. The famous Rialto Bridge, for example, is one of the most iconic symbols of Venice. It was originally built in the 16th century as a marketplace but now serves as a pedestrian bridge. Despite its beauty, Venice faces many challenges, including rising sea levels and overcrowding. As a result, the city's authorities are working to preserve its heritage while also finding ways to manage the impact of tourism.",
